<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur St. Clair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1737">1737</a>–<a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arthur St. Clair (1737–1818) was a Scottish-born American soldier and politician who served as a general in the Revolutionary War, as president of the Confederation Congress in 1787 and as first governor of the Northwest Territory. In November 1791 his army was routed near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wabash-river">Wabash River</a> by the Western Confederacy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-turtle">Little Turtle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-jacket">Blue Jacket</a>, the worst defeat ever suffered by the US Army against Native Americans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-st-clair.jpg" alt="Arthur St. Clair" width="320" height="366" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Willson Peale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ArthurStClairOfficialPortrait-restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arthur St. Clair was born in Thurso, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, on 23 March 1737 by the modern calendar, probably the son of William Sinclair, a merchant, and Elizabeth Balfour. He reportedly attended the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> and was apprenticed to the physician William Hunter. In 1757 he bought a commission in the Royal American Regiment and served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-and-indian-war">French and Indian War</a>, at the capture of Louisburg under General Jeffery Amherst and under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wolfe">James Wolfe</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-plains-of-abraham">Battle of the Plains of Abraham</a>. He married Phoebe Bayard in 1760, resigned his commission in 1762 and settled in the Ligonier Valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, where his mills made him the largest landowner west of the Allegheny Mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In January 1776 he became colonel of the 3rd Pennsylvania Regiment, fought at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trois-rivieres">Trois-Rivières</a> and took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>'s crossing of the Delaware on 25–26 December 1776; many biographers credit him with the plan that led to the capture of Princeton. Promoted major general in February 1777, he was given Fort Ticonderoga and on 5 July 1777 abandoned it to the larger army of General John Burgoyne. A court-martial in 1778 cleared him, but he never held a command again in the war.</p><p>Elected President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a> on 2 February 1787, he presided while it passed the Northwest Ordinance. As governor of the Northwest Territory he named Cincinnati after the Society of the Cincinnati and issued Maxwell's Code, the territory's first written laws. The Treaty of Fort Harmar of 1789 was rejected by the tribes as illegitimate, and in the war that followed St. Clair, made senior officer of the army in March 1791, led the expedition that ended in his defeat of 4 November 1791, in which more than 600 American soldiers and scores of camp followers were killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cleared by an investigation, he gave up his commission in March 1792 at Washington's request and remained governor. A Federalist, he angered the Ohio Democrat-Republicans, and President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a> removed him in 1802; the first Ohio Constitution gave the state a weak governor largely in reaction to his rule. He died in poverty in Greensburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, on 31 August 1818. St. Clair County in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, Missouri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> and St. Clairsville in Ohio are among the places named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arthur St. Clair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It defeated Josiah Harmar in 1790 and destroyed <b>Arthur St. Clair's</b> army on the Wabash in 1791.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-confederacy">Western Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thurso, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Greensburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=58.6,-3.52&amp;year=1789" aria-label="Thurso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4475 189 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Arthur St. Clair"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4925 459h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4925 459h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=58.6,-3.52&amp;year=1789">What was here in 1789? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
